WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.670
- I think that what a lot of
people are struggling with here

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:05.880
is that, you know, you
were an MIT physics major.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:06.713
- Yep.

00:00:06.713 --> 00:00:09.090
- You are, by all
accounts, a very smart guy.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.670
How is it possible that
you overlooked this

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.650
more than $5 billion problem?

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:17.730
- I ask myself a lot how I
made a series of mistakes

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.130
that seem, they don't just seem dumb,

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:24.120
they seem like the type of
mistakes I could see myself

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.550
having ridiculed someone
else for having maked,

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:30.270
the kind of mistakes that I
would've considered myself

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:31.795
to have been above.

00:00:31.795 --> 00:00:34.295
(tense music)

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:44.400
- As we've learned about what happened

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.630
over the past few weeks,

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.300
we've learned that there
were some issues involving

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.940
where customer funds were put for FTX

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.740
and some accounting issues.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:56.460
Can you walk us through what
the problem was on that side?

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.877
- Right, you rewind to 2019, 2020.

00:00:59.877 --> 00:01:03.300
FTX didn't have bank accounts,
and that was because as

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:04.980
a global crypto exchange at the time,

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.990
almost none of them were able to get,

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:08.340
you know, global banking.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.530
Some customers still wanted to onboard

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:11.730
fiat currencies though.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:14.850
You know, FTX was maintaining
cryptocurrency wallets

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:19.230
but not banking accounts,
and so some of them

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:22.800
would wire money to Alameda and then asked

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:24.913
to be credited on their FTX account.

00:01:24.913 --> 00:01:27.413
(tense music)

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.760
- How much of this
customer money was wired

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.730
to the Alameda bank accounts?

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:40.740
- I don't have the numbers in front of me

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:45.740
but my estimation is,
I think more than half.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:46.680
- More than half.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.680
- More than half of
Alameda's total position

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.990
came via that method.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.150
- So how many billions of
dollars are we talking about,

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:55.170
at least ballpark?

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:56.760
- I think north of five.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.310
- And so this was actually
US dollars that people,

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:01.452
customers transferred to
Alameda bank accounts.

00:02:01.452 --> 00:02:02.285
- Yeah.

00:02:02.285 --> 00:02:04.230
- And they were credited for it on FTX.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:05.730
- Yeah, that's right.

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.400
- So more than $5 billion were transferred

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.470
into these Alameda bank accounts.

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:11.490
- Yep.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:15.150
- It was then credited
so that customers saw

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:16.470
that they had that balance

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:18.120
on their screens.
- [Sam] Yep.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.100
- What actually happened to the dollars

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:21.750
sitting in those bank accounts?

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:25.110
- One phrasing of it one
could use would be to say

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.840
that Alameda effectively
sent those dollars

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.880
from its FTX account to the user,

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.070
but that's a ledger transfer of course.

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:35.640
Outside of that, the answer
is they were wired to Alameda.

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:38.370
I can only speculate about
what happened after that.

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:41.070
- So you don't know what
happened to those $5 billion?

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.710
- They were, I mean, they
were wired to Alameda.

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:48.710
I wasn't running Alameda,
but I can now go back

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.630
and take a guess at what, you know,

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:55.530
where they were ultimately
spent or used or something,

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:57.930
but dollars are fungible with
each other, and so it's not

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:01.620
like this $1 bill over here
that you can trace through

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:02.970
from start to finish.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.400
What you get is more just omnibus pots

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.289
of assets of various forms.

00:03:07.289 --> 00:03:09.789
(tense music)

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.500
- Was there double counting going on?

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:20.010
- There was not double
counting from FTX financials.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:25.010
FTX financials were correctly,
effectively debiting

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:29.520
this amount from total
customer balances on FTX.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.550
- So let's say Bob wires $100 to one of

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.590
these Alameda bank accounts.

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:36.090
Bob checks his FTX screen.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:37.731
FTX has credited his account.

00:03:37.731 --> 00:03:38.564
- [Sam] That's right.

00:03:38.564 --> 00:03:39.397
- That says he's got $100.

00:03:39.397 --> 00:03:40.230
- Yep.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:41.876
- But behind the scenes, Alameda goes

00:03:41.876 --> 00:03:43.750
and does something with that money.

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:48.427
- Yeah, Alameda has negative
one, was it $100 that?

00:03:48.427 --> 00:03:49.350
- $100, yeah.

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:50.183
- Yeah, yeah.

00:03:50.183 --> 00:03:54.000
You know, Alameda then should
have negative $100 on FTX,

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:57.600
right, because effectively,
or at least that would be

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.420
one way to mechanic this,
right, would be Bob sends

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:04.020
to Alameda over here off of
FTX through wire transfer,

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:06.180
then on FTX, Alameda sends to Bob,

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:09.210
but the whole key there
is you need to keep track

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:12.150
of that negative Alameda
piece, of the fact

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:14.130
that Alameda is crediting other people.

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:17.910
Thus, that should be
debiting from the funds

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:20.070
that they have on FTX.

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:24.510
- So this problem developed
in which $5 billion,

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:27.750
more than $5 billion of user
funds was sent to Alameda

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:31.110
and it was not properly
seen in the systems.

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:33.513
It made Alameda's position seem larger.

00:04:33.513 --> 00:04:35.160
- Less larger than it was.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.160
- You only became aware at
the beginning of November.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:39.240
- I don't remember the exact date,

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:41.070
but it is somewhere around there, yeah.

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:43.320
I was surprised at what happened

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:45.330
when you added those numbers together.

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:49.950
I don't know exactly when
that notion became so large,

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:51.360
that wire transfer piece.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.630
I think it was surprisingly recently,

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:56.426
given that it was, because this was

00:04:56.426 --> 00:04:59.460
a sort of legacy system, right?

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:02.160
We transitioned away from
this system over time,

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:04.528
but I think that some
people just kept wiring

00:05:04.528 --> 00:05:08.490
to Alameda anyway, even
after we had built out

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:10.640
our own payment networks
and removed any traces

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:13.830
of that old pathway, and
so I think it was maybe

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:18.780
surprisingly recently that a
lot of that happened as well.

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:23.130
- Was Alameda using this money
that came in to that account

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:26.073
as part of its collateral on FTX?

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:31.470
- I don't, not to my
knowledge, but I'm not sure.

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:34.420
- But that is possible that
that was how it was being used.

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:39.750
- I don't know what they,
you know, what they did

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:43.980
with which pieces of assets
and given that dollars

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:46.260
are fungible with each
other at the end of the day,

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:48.720
I'm not sure it's even
well defined to talk about

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.030
whether it was those
dollars or other dollars

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:52.350
that were sent somewhere.

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:54.030
- But if a dollar went in.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:54.863
- Yep.

00:05:54.863 --> 00:05:55.852
- To one of those Alameda bank accounts.

00:05:55.852 --> 00:05:56.940
- Yep.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:05:59.220
- Then it would've become
part of the customer's

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:02.373
margin position and part of
Alameda's margin position.

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:07.740
- It would've, if it had gone
to an Alameda bank account

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:10.140
then it would become part
of the customer's collateral

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:12.630
on FTX, right, and it would've increased

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:16.020
Alameda's margin position
on FTX effectively.

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:18.520
(tense music)

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.420
- I wanted to ask about FTX terms of use.

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:31.253
- Yep.

00:06:31.253 --> 00:06:33.450
- They say that you
control the digital assets

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:34.283
held in your account.

00:06:34.283 --> 00:06:37.440
They can't be loaned out,
except there's also another part

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:39.510
of the terms of use that
talk about margin trading.

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:40.343
- Yep.

00:06:40.343 --> 00:06:42.900
- In which there is
actually language that says,

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:44.250
your money could be loaned out.

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:45.213
- Right.

00:06:45.213 --> 00:06:46.479
- And it could disappear if
somebody's trading goes bad.

00:06:46.479 --> 00:06:47.312
- Right.

00:06:47.312 --> 00:06:51.377
- On FTX, did you have to
opt in to do margin trading?

00:06:51.377 --> 00:06:54.120
- So for the peer-to-peer borrow lending

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:55.620
order book, you did, yes.

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:59.160
- How many people and how much
money was actually in that?

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:00.840
- I don't know exactly
off the top of my head.

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:03.060
I think it was, I wanna say,

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:05.280
eight or $10 billion at the peak.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:07.080
I don't know what it is today.

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:09.677
- Were you sweeping
people into margin trading

00:07:09.677 --> 00:07:14.100
and allowing their
collateral to be loaned out?

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:16.470
- I honestly, off the top of
my head, I don't know exactly

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:19.237
what procedures we used to
determine whether an account was,

00:07:19.237 --> 00:07:21.780
you know, doing margin trading or,

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:26.758
I do remember, you know, a way
that people could have opted

00:07:26.758 --> 00:07:30.240
into it, but I don't know
off the top of my head.

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:32.440
- Was there a violation
of the terms of use?

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:37.777
- I don't know of a violation
of the terms of use.

00:07:37.777 --> 00:07:40.410
I don't know every line
of the terms of use.

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:42.570
I can't confidently say there wasn't,

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:46.177
but I don't know of one.

00:07:46.177 --> 00:07:48.677
(tense music)

00:07:56.310 --> 00:08:01.310
- In some of the court
filings, the new head of FTX

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:05.340
has said that there was a
secret exemption for Alameda

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:08.040
from the normal
auto-liquidation protocols,

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:09.660
which some people have called a backdoor.

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:10.895
- Right.

00:08:10.895 --> 00:08:12.030
- Is, can you tell us about
this secret exemption?

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:15.630
- I don't know exactly what
was being referred to there.

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:17.610
I have, that has not
been communicated to me

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:20.190
beyond the public statements, nor was I

00:08:20.190 --> 00:08:22.290
consulted or asked on that.

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:23.992
So I can only speculate about what

00:08:23.992 --> 00:08:26.820
was being referred to there.

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:29.370
My guess is that that
may have been referring

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:32.919
to wires sent to Alameda, I'm not sure.

00:08:32.919 --> 00:08:37.919
I, it may, I, yeah, I can't
speculate on exactly what,

00:08:38.114 --> 00:08:41.010
what he was referring to there.

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:43.362
- But could Alameda be auto-liquidated

00:08:43.362 --> 00:08:46.683
like any other customer?

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:51.030
- I don't, again have access
to all this right now.

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:56.030
I believe that there was a
system in place that I think

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:00.173
Alameda and some other accounts were using

00:09:00.173 --> 00:09:04.530
which did have some liquidation policies,

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:07.950
but which were specific
for larger positions.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:10.650
I actually don't know the details of that.

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:13.710
These were things that I
think like, account managers

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:17.310
and legal were working out
together over the last year

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:21.030
and I was not deeply
involved in that process.

00:09:21.030 --> 00:09:24.900
I do think that the larger
accounts did have some amount

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:28.260
of you know, extra margin
capacity in some cases.

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:30.510
- So Alameda could take on riskier trades

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:32.730
than other FTX customers?

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:34.500
- I'm not sure, but I think
there may have been cases

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:38.580
where at least they could
take on larger positions.

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:41.370
- How is it that as a
90% owner of the firm

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:44.280
and founder of the firm who
was close with the leadership,

00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:45.750
you didn't know what was going on?

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:47.940
- The thing is, I was busy.

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:50.520
I was, you know, FTX was a full-time job.

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:53.557
It was more than a full-time
job and I didn't have

00:09:53.557 --> 00:09:56.910
enough brain cycles left
to understand everything

00:09:56.910 --> 00:09:59.220
going on at Alameda if I wanted to.

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:01.530
I also didn't want to
because I was concerned

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:05.070
about conflicts of interest
and I felt like it would be

00:10:05.070 --> 00:10:08.170
inappropriate for me to
be, you know, looped into,

00:10:08.170 --> 00:10:11.280
you know, certainly to details
of what was going on there.

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:16.280
It caused me to not dig in
very much, to not try to engage

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:17.954
very much on what Alameda was doing.

00:10:17.954 --> 00:10:22.954
It was a huge oversight,
obviously, but, you know,

00:10:26.250 --> 00:10:31.250
the honest answer is, I mean,
obviously I should have been.

00:10:31.590 --> 00:10:33.100
- Sam, what you've described sounds

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:36.757
at a minimum like gross negligence.

00:10:36.757 --> 00:10:39.330
Obviously there are a lot of people there

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:41.530
who think that you
committed outright fraud.

00:10:42.515 --> 00:10:44.613
Do you expect to go to prison?

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:49.170
- I'm not focusing on that right now.

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:50.520
I don't think I, you know,

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:52.740
tried to commit fraud anywhere here.

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:54.630
I was trying to do the best I could.

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:57.637
Obviously that wasn't nearly
as good as I thought it was,

00:10:57.637 --> 00:11:01.860
but honestly it's not what
I'm focusing on right now.

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:04.440
I don't, this is about the
customers at the end of the day.

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:06.780
Like that's, there's one of me.

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:11.070
There are millions of them,
and I don't think it's right

00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:13.800
for me to be focusing on
what happens to me here.

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:15.630
I think I should be focusing

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:18.873
on what can be done for customers.

